[38;5;10mdriver(1, 2, 2, -5)   C: "1 2 0 -5"   Rust (before): "1 2 1 -5"
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I confirmed the [38;5;10m& 1[0m semantics are not an artifact of the unoptimized CMake default by rebuilding the C at [38;5;10m-O0[0m/[38;5;10m-O1[0m/[38;5;10m-O2[0m/[38;5;10m-O3[0m/[38;5;10m-Os[0m and probing through [38;5;10mdlopen[0m — all five give [38;5;10mb=2 → 0[0m, [38;5;10mb=3 → 1[0m. Fixed in [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m to [38;5;10m(b & 1) != 0[0m, which is identical for canonical [38;5;10m0[0m/[38;5;10m1[0m and now matches C for all 256 byte values.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Everything else in the translation was already correct: the packed [38;5;10m{bits: c_uint, z: c_int}[0m layout matches the C bit-field allocation unit, the [38;5;10m%u[0m/[38;5;10m%d[0m promotions match, and both [38;5;10m.so[0m files export exactly [38;5;10mdriver[0m and [38;5;10mprint_foo[0m.[0m[0m
